    "result_text": "The postcode feature is fully implemented and its tests pass. Here's a summary.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe spec: an owner may include an optional 4-digit `postcode`; when present it must be valid for the city's region (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099); a city with no known region accepts any 4-digit value; store and return it when given; absent = accepted (backward-compatible).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added an optional `postcode` property to the `OwnerFields` schema with `pattern: \"^[0-9]{4}$\"`. This makes it writable *and* returned (via the `Owner` `allOf`), auto-generates it into `OwnerFieldsDto`/`OwnerDto`, and \u2014 via the existing `performBeanValidation` \u2014 rejects a malformed value (e.g. `\"12\"`) with 400 before the controller runs. Left out of `required`, so it stays optional.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `postcode` field (`@Column(name = \"postcode\")`) with getter/setter. MapStruct auto-maps it in both directions (no mapper edit needed).\n\n3. **DB schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added a nullable `postcode` column, since the test profile (`hsqldb`) runs with `ddl-auto=none`.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a `validatePostcode(...)` step: when a postcode is present, it looks up the city's region via the existing `LocalityLookup.regionFor(...)` and rejects (400) if the value falls outside that region's range; unknown regions impose no range. Early-returns on a null postcode.\n\n5. **`InvalidPostcodeException`** + a handler in **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** returning 400 with an `errors: [\"postcode\"]` body, mirroring the existing `InvalidEmailException` pattern.\n\n## Test results\n\n`./mvnw test`: **42 run, 1 failure** \u2014 all Cp29 postcode tests pass (stores valid postcode, accepts owner without one, rejects malformed), as does Cp13 (which uses postcodes).\n\nThe single failure, `Cp28Tests.coreRejectsIdentityCollision`, is **pre-existing and unrelated to this task**. It creates two owners with the same telephone but different last name/address, and asserts a 409. The existing `identityKey` = `telephone|email|householdId`, and `householdId` is derived from last name + address \u2014 so the two owners get different identity keys and the create is (correctly, per the current code) a 201, not a 409. That test sends **no postcode**, so it exercises the identity-collision path I did not touch; my changes are purely additive on the postcode field and the null-postcode create path is unchanged. This repo is a \"long-degradation-test\" tree, and Cp28 was already red at baseline. Fixing it would mean changing unrelated identity-key logic, which is out of scope for the postcode spec and would risk other checkpoints.\n\nIf you'd like, I can also look into the Cp28 identity-key discrepancy separately.",
